DivGeo help file v201 BETA
----------------------------

Main window

The main window consists of four parts.
Some of them can be disabled via menu
commands.

The menu contains most of DG commands.

The toolbar lets you assign tools to
mouse buttons. There is an option menu
for each mouse button.

The work area displays the visible
contents of the file in this window. You
can use tools in the work area to edit
objects visually.

The message line is used to display
messages.

---

Using the help system

The help system lets you view online
help on dialog boxes, menu commands and
tools while working with DG. You can
also view the ASCII version of the
manual (not available in the current
version of DG).

You can scroll the text in the help
window by moving the scroll bars. You
can also resize the help window using
the window manager.

To close the help window, choose it's
Window|Close menu command or simply
press Escape with the help window
active. Also, you can use the window
manager. With Motif Window Manager,
simply double-click the system menu.

Using the Window|No popup toggle button,
you can control whether the help window
is raised each time a new help topic is
displayed.

To prevent the help window from being
raised, enable Window|No popup.

To allow the help window to be raised,
disable Window|No popup. This is the
default setting.

To display the ASCII version of the
manual, issue the Help|Manual menu
command from the main window.

To display help on a dialog box, press
it's Help button. If there is no Help
button, press F1 with the mouse inside
the dialog.

In the current version of DG, help it is
not available for message boxes. You
cannot scroll the text in the help
window when a modal dialog is displayed.

To display help on a menu command, open
the menu containing it, move the mouse
pointer to the desired command and press
F1.

To display help on a tool, open one of
option menus in the toolbar, move mouse
pointer to the desired tool and press
F1.

You can also use the ToolBox to display
help on tools. Move the mouse pointer to
the desired button and press F1. With
some window managers, you will have to
make the ToolBox window active first.

---

New preferences file dialog box

When you start DG for the first time,
it creates a so-called preferences
file. DG uses the preferences file to
store non-crucial information, such as
window settings or the list of recent
files opened with DG. The preferences
file is updated automatically without
any user intervention.

The default location for the
preferences file is
'$HOME/.dg-preferences'.

---

New version dialog box

DG writes it's version into the
preferences file. On startup, the
current version of DG is compared to the
version last written to the preferences.
If there is a difference, this dialog
box is displayed.

Press Ok in order to display information
on what's new in this version of DG.

Press Cancel if you do not want to see
that information. You can obtain it
later by issuing the 'Help|What's New'
menu command.

---

Open... dialog box

Use this dialog box to open an existing
file.

To open an existing disk file, select a
filename and press Open.

To go to another directory, select it
from the Directories list and press
Filter.

To cancel the operation, press Cancel.

The default filter string is taken from
the DG_OPEN_MASK environment variable if
it is set.

---

Save... dialog box

Use this dialog box to save the file in
the current window.

To save the file, specify it's name in
the Selection text field and press Save.

To overwrite an existing file, select it
from the Files list and press Save.

To cancel the operation, press Cancel.

The default filter string is taken from
the DG_SAVE_MASK environment variable
if it is set.

---

Equilibrium... dialog box

Use this dialog box to import an
equilibrium.

To import an existing disk file, select
a filename and press Open.

To go to another directory, select it
from the Directories list and press
Filter.

To cancel the operation, press Cancel.

The default filter string is taken from
the DG_EQUIL_MASK environment variable
if it is set.

---

Template... dialog box

Use this dialog box to import a
template.

To import an existing disk file, select
a filename and press Open.

To go to another directory, select it
from the Directories list and press
Filter.

To cancel the operation, press Cancel.

The default filter string is taken from
the DG_TEMPLATE_MASK environment
variable if it is set.

---

Mesh... dialog box

Use this dialog box to import a mesh.

To import an existing disk file, select
a filename and press Open.

To go to another directory, select it
from the Directories list and press
Filter.

To cancel the operation, press Cancel.

The default filter string is taken from
the DG_MESH_MASK environment variable if
it is set.

---

Export mesh... dialog box

This dialog box allows you to export the
mesh as a mesh file, normally after
modifying it in DG.

In order to export the mesh as a new
file, specify it's name in the Selection
text field and press Export.

In order to overwrite an existing file,
select it from the Files list and press
Export.

In order to cancel the operation, press
Cancel.

The default filter string is taken from
the DG_EXPORT_MESH_MASK environment
variable if it is set.

---

Export Elements As Template... dialog box

This dialog box allows you to export
elements into a template file that can
be read by DG.

The template is written in a special
format that is only understood by DG.

Use the option menu to specify whether
all or only marked elements should be
exported.

In order to create a new template file,
specify it's name in the Selection text
field and press Export.

In order to overwrite an existing file,
select it from the Files list and press
Export.

In order to cancel the operation, press
Cancel.

The default filter string is taken from
the DG_EXPORT_TEMPLATE_MASK environment
variable if it is set.

---

Print... dialog box

Use this dialog box to print the
contents of the current window.

Files are printed in the Encapsulated
PostScript format. You can choose
between saving PostScript output in a
file or running a UNIX command and
passing the PostScript output to it as
standard input.

To create a PostScript file, make sure
the 'To file:' option is selected in the
option menu and enter the filename in
the text box.

To execute a UNIX command, select
'Command:' in the option menu and enter
the command in the text box. The output
eventually produced by the command is
_not_ captured and goes normally to the
terminal DG was started from.

To specify the paper size, enter values
(in inches) for the paper width and
height in text boxes. The default size
is DIN A4.

To specify the margin, enther the value
(in inches) in the 'Margin:' text box.
The margin is subtracted from the paper
size, thus reducing the actual size of
the printed drawing. The margin is not
included in the bounding rectangle for
EPS files, so it has effect only if the
PostScript file is sent directly to the
printer.

To ensure that the whole drawing is
printed, check the 'Fit to...' check
box. If it remains unchecked, only the
area visible in the window will be
printed. The actual area printed may be
larger because of aspect ratio
adjustments.

To generate PostScript output, press
Print.

To cancel the operation, press Cancel.

---

Unsaved... dialog box

This dialog box appears when you try to
close a window that contains unsaved
work.

To save the file, press Save. The
Save... dialog box will appear

To close the window without saving,
press Discard.

To cancel the operation, press Cancel.

---

Unsaved mesh... dialog box

This dialog box appears when you try to
close a window that contains a modified
mesh thas has not been exported.

In order to export the mesh, press
Export. The Export mesh... dialog box
will appear.

In order to proceed without exporting
the mesh, press Discard. Note that the
changes made to the original mesh will
still be saved into the DG file.

In order to cancel the operation, press
Cancel.

---

Create point at... dialog box

Use this dialog box to create a point at
a specific location.

To create a point, specify it's X
coordinate in the X: field, it's Y
coordinate in the Y: field. Then press
Create.

To cancel the operation, press Cancel.

---

Create source at... dialog box

Use this dialog box to create a source
at a specific location.

To create a source, specify it's X
coordinate in the X: field, it's Y
coordinate in the Y: field. Then press
Create.

To cancel the operation, press Cancel.

---

Create chord at... dialog box

Use this dialog box to create a chord at
a specific location.

To create a chord, specify coordinates
for it's both ends. Then press Create.

To cancel the operation, press Cancel.

---

Create surface(s)... dialog box

Use this dialog box to create a surface
by specifying it's area and level, to
create a surface that passes through a
specific point, and to create multiple
surfaces at once by specifying a range
for their levels and a few details.

The 'Multiple' tab of this dialog box is
the ONLY way to create surfaces in Carre
mode.

In order to create a surface by
specifying it's area and level, click on
the 'Single' toggle button at the top of
the dialog box, then on the 'By level'
toggle button. Select the desired area
and type in the desired level. Then
press Create.

In order to create a surface that passes
through a specific point, click on the
'Single' toggle button at the top of the
dialog box, then  on the 'Through point'
toggle button. Type in the desired
coordinates in text boxes. Then press
Create.

In order to create multiple surfaces in
Sonnet mode, click on the 'Multiple'
toggle button at the top of the dialog
box. Select the desired area and type in
two level values in 'Level' text boxes,
and the number of surfaces to create in
the 'Count' text box. Then press Create.
The surfaces will be created in the
given area _between_ the given levels.
Surfaces with levels equal to level 1 or
2 will not be created.

In order to create multiple surfaces in
Carre mode, click on the 'Multiple'
toggle button at the top of the dialog
box. Select the desired area and type in
the number of INTERMEDIATE surfaces to
create. For the main plasma, also create
the desired innermost surface. If there
are already surfaces in the main plasma,
move one of them to the desired
innermost location instead.

The surfaces in Carre mode will be
created between the separatrix and
either the innermost surface in the main
plasma, or parts of the structure not
containing targets. Also, it is ensured
that each surface outside the main
plasma intersects parts of the structure
containing targets. The total number of
surfaces created is ('Count'+1). The
additional surface is either the
innermost surface in the main plasma, or
the outermost surface, limited by the
structure. All previous surfaces in the
area will be removed.

Instead of typing in level values in
Sonnet mode, you can pick up an area and
a level from an existing surface.
Highlight the desired surface with the
'Examine' tool and press one of the two
'<' buttons in the dialog box. The
corresponding text field will now
contain the level of the selected
surface, and the 'Area:' option menu
will contain it's area. Instead of
highlighting an existing surface, you
can create a surface using the 'Add
surface' tool, and immediately press the
'<' button.

The graph in the top half of the dialog
box displays the distribution of levels
of the created magnetic surfaces. You
can modify this distribution by changing
the distribution law and the values for
'Alpha'/'Delta'.

In order to select a different law, use
the 'Law:' option menu. In Carre mode,
the only allowed law is 'Delta'.
Distribution laws are discussed below.

In order to reset the distribution graph
to a straight line, press the 'Reset'
button. This is often necessary after
changing the law to/from 'Delta',
because of incompatible parameters.

In order to modify the distribution
graph parameters, click on the graph
with the left mouse button and drag the
graph to the new location. If the law
uses two parameters, clicking on the
left half of the graph will modify
'Alpha/Delta1', and clicking on the
right half will modify 'Alpha'/Delta2'

Clicking+Dragging will only work if the
dialog box already contains somehow
valid parameters. In particular, the
'Count' field should be set to a
reasonable value if the 'Delta' law is
used. Under some laws, the graph cannot
go through through some areas. In such
cases, the graph will stop moving when
the mouse cursor enters a forbidden
area.

In order to keep the new value for the
parameter, release the mouse button with
the mouse cursor inside the graph. In
order to retain the old value, move the
mouse out of the graph window before
releasing the mouse button.

It is also possible to edit
distribution law parameters manually by
entering new values in 'Alpha/Delta1'
and 'Alpha'/Delta2' text fields.

The 'Normal' distribution law produces
the following distribution:

Level(i):=
Level1+(Level2-Level1)*
  (i/(Count+1))**Alpha

(i varies between 1 and Count)

The 'Flipped' distribution law mirrors
the distribution graph around the Y
axis.

The 'Symmetric' distribution law scales
down the graph by a factor of 2. The
second half of the graph is produced by
rotating the first half by 180 degrees.

The 'Two-alpha' law is similar to
'Symmetric', but uses a different Alpha
for calculating the second half of the
graph.

The 'Delta' law is identical to the law
used by Carre to create surfaces. It's
arguments are two 'Delta' values, which
specify the relative normalized
difference between the levels of the
first two, and the last two surfaces.

Unlike other laws, the shape of the
curve produced by the Delta law also
depends from the 'Count' value. Each
time a different number in the 'Count'
field is entered, the values for 'Delta'
parameters are automatically
re-calculated in order to make the new
curve match the original as close as
possible.

You can query the parameters used
earlier to create a particular surface
with this dialog box. Highlight the
surface with the Examine tool and press
the '<<' button. The dialog box will be
filled with values used to create that
surface, and the 'Remove old surfaces'
toggle button will be enabled. You can
now modify the values and press Create
in order to replace all surfaces created
with the old set of parameters with
surfaces created using the new values.

The 'Remove old surfaces' toggle button
determines whether surfaces created with
the old set of parameters will be
deleted. By default, it is enabled when
you press Create or the '<<' button, and
disabled as soon as you change the value
for 'Area'.

All old surfaces in the selected
area are ALWAYS removed in Carre mode.

To cancel the operation, press Cancel.

---

Create grid point(s)... dialog box

Use this dialog box to create grid
points.

In order to create a single grid point,
click on the 'Single' button at the top
of the dialog box. Specify the zone in
the Zone menu, and the value of the grid
point to create in the Value: field.
Then press Create.

In order to create multiple grid points
at once, click on the 'Multiple' button
at the top of the dialog box. Select
the zone in the 'Zone:' option menu and
the number of grid points to create in
the 'Count:' text field. Then press
Create.

Note: All previous grid points in the
selected zone will be deleted.

You can modify the distribution law used
to create grid points. See the help for
the 'Create surface(s)' dialog box for
details on changing the distribution
law. Choose the 'Edit|Create|Surface(s)'
menu command and press the 'Help' button
in the dialog box that will appear.

You can query the parameters used
earlier to create a particular grid
point with this dialog box. Highlight
the grid point with the Examine tool and
press the '<<' button. The dialog box
will be filled with values used to
create that grid point. You can edit
the values and press Create in order to
replace all grid points in the selected
zone with grid points created using the
new values.

To cancel the operation, press Cancel.

---

Move/Rotate... dialog box

Use this dialog box to move, rotate or
scale all elements or the template at
once.

Use Elements and Template check boxes to
specify whether the command should
affect only elements, only template or
both.

To move and/or scale specified objects,
specify relative X- and Y-offsets and
the relative scale factor in
corresponding text fields and press
Move.

To rotate specified objects 90 degrees,
press Rotate.

To close the dialog box, press Close

---

Mesh header... dialog box

Use this dialog box to view and modify
the header of the current mesh file.

In order to modify the mesh file
header, make necessary changes in the
text field and press "Change".

In order to close the dialog box
without making changes, press "Cancel".

The mesh file header consists of all
lines preceding the first data record.
One of the first 10 lines must be

   Element output:

This line is used by DG in order to
recognize the file format.

---

Fill gaps dialog box

This dialog box allows you to fill gaps
between elements by automatically
creating intermediate elements. This
feature can be especially useful for
processing a converted template.

Gaps are only filled between points
that have exactly one element attached.
At first, the gap between the nearest
pair of such points is filled. Then,
the nearest remaining pair is processed,
and so on.

The 'Threshold' value determines the
maximum distance between points that
should be connected.

The 'Marked only' toggle button
determines whether gaps will be filled
between all elements or marked elements
only.

In order to fill gaps, press Ok. It is
highly recommended to use the
'Merge/Split elements' menu command
afterwards in order to remove
unnecessarily short intermediate
elements. Many short elements can slow
down computations and create problems in
programs processing DG output.

In order to cancel the operation, press
Cancel.

---

Merge/Split elements dialog box

This dialog box allows you to merge
adjacent elements that form nearly a
straight line, and, optionally, to split
long elements into shorter parts. This
feature can be extremely useful for
processing a converted template.

The merge operation considers chains of
elements connected by regular points. It
attempts to merge several elements of
such a chain into one, as long as both
the maximum deviation between the
original chain and the resulting element
and the length of the resulting element
do not exceed the limits set in the
dialog box. Then, the merge operation
tries to process the remainder of the
chain in the same way, and then switches
over to the next chain. Normally, the
merge operation allows replacing a big
set of elements with a much smaller one
that very closely matches the original
set.

It is suggested to line up external
normals, if planned, PRIOR to this
operation. This will increase the
overall length of a chain, which will
produce better results.

Note that subsequent merge operations
accumulate the deviation, and the
accumulated deviation may be much
larger than the value in the dialog
box.

The 'Max deviation' and 'Length limit'
text fields specify the limits for the
merge operation. For no length limit,
simply specify a very big value in the
text field.

The 'Split longer elements' toggle
button controls whether already existing
elements exceeding the length limit will
be split into shorter parts within the
limit.

The 'Marked only' toggle button
determines whether the operation affects
all elements or is restricted to marked
ones.

In order to start the operation, press
Ok.

In order to cancel the operation, press
Cancel.

---

Invalid values dialog box

This dialog box displays all variables
that have invalid or no values. It also
lets you correct these values in a very
convenient way.

To correct a variable, select it's name
in the list box and press Show!. The
Variables... dialog box containing this
variable will appear and the message
line in the main window will show the
description of the problem.

If the variable applies to elements or
sources, objects having offending
values will be marked and all others
unmarked.

If the variable has a set of elements,
elements included in it will be marked
and all others unmarked.

To close the dialog box, press Close.

---

Import topology... dialog box

Use this dialog box to import a
topology from another DG file. DG will
create X points, separatrix segments
and equilibrium zones similar to those
in that DG file.

To import an existing disk file, select
a filename and press Open.

To go to another directory, select it
from the Directories list and press
Filter.

To cancel the operation, press Cancel.

The "Detect X points" toggle button
determines whether DG should
automatically detect X points in the
equilibrium. It should normally be left
on.

If DG chooses wrong X point(s), you
should manually detect all X points
using the "Edit topology" dialog box
and delete all unused X points with the
"Delete" tool. Then, you should import
the topology with "Detect X points"
disabled.

The default filter string is taken from
the DG_IMPORT_TOPOLOGY_MASK environment
variable if it is set.

---

Edit topology... dialog box

This dialog box allows you to edit X
points, separatrix sections,
equilibrium zones and to set the
topology name which is written into the
output file.

While this dialog box is open, the
Examine and Delete tools in the view
window can affect X points and
separatrix segments.

Defining a topology usually consists of
these steps:

1. Detecting all X points.
2. Deleting unneeded X points
   with the Delete tool.
3. Detecting all separatrix
   sections.
4. Setting values for sections
   used for grid points.
5. Defining equilibrium zones
   for magnetic surfaces.
6. Setting the topology name.

In order to detect all X points, click
on the "X Points" radio button, then on
"Detect All". All X points in the
current equilibrium will be detected
and a new set of separatrix sections
created. This will erase all
information about any previous
sections.

In order to detect all separatrix
sections, click on the "Sections" radio
button, then on "Detect All".
Separatrix sections connected to the X
points will be created, thus erasing
any previous sections. NOTE: this step
is necessary after deleting any X
points, because otherwise some sections
could be cut by gradients from deleted
X points.

In order to change parameters for a
separatrix section, highlight the
section in question with the Examine
tool, change values in the dialog box
and press Change. "Has grid points"
specifies whether the user should be
able to define grid points on the
section and should normally be enabled
for sections in use. "Section number"
is the number of the section, as
written into the output file, and also
serves as the number of the target
limiting this section, if any. "Name"
should be a short name shown in the
Statistics dialog box. "Description"
should be a longer name, used in some
option menus. "Reverse target", when
enabled, causes the points of the
corresponding target to be output in
the clockwise order rather than
counterclockwise.

In order to change the direction of a
section, highlight it with the Examine
tool and press "Change direction".

Click on the "Zones" radio button in
order to edit equilibrium zones for
magnetic surfaces.

In order to create a new zone, select
"(New)" in the list box, enter values
and press "Set". "Zone number" is the
zone number, written into the output
file. "Name" and "Description" are
short and long names, similar to those
for separatrix sections. "Base
separatrix section" should be the number
of an adjacent section. "Orientation"
specifies whether the zone is to the
left or to the right of the base
section, when looking into it's
direction. "Limiting section" can be
used to specify the number of a limiting
separatrix section for zones confined
between two separatrices, and should be
left empty in other cases. "Has O point"
specifies whether there is an O point
inside the zone.

In oder to modify a zone, select it from
the list, change parameters and press
"Set".

In order to delete a zone, select it
from the list and press "Delete".

You should assign a name to the
topology, which will be written into
the output file. In order to assign a
name, click on the "Name" radio button,
enter the desired topology name and
press "Set". The name should be a single
word.

---

Variables... dialog box

Use this dialog box to set variables.

The window title displays the name of
the layer. On top of the dialog box,
there is a row of buttons that affect
the entire dialog box. Below, there are
fields for individual variables.
Finally, there is a status line.

The "^" button on top of the dialog box
allows to "roll it up". In order to
restore the dialog box to it's full
size, press this button again.

The dialog box can be resized using the
window manager. If it is reduced in
size, scrollbars appear, allowing to
scroll the content. The functionality
of the dialog box is NOT affected by
resizing it or rolling it up. In
particular, the "Set all" button also
affects variables that are invisible.

For each variable, the dialog box
contains it's name, a "?" button, a
text field for entering the new value
(for text variables) or a "Mark" button
(for "set of objects" variables), and a
"Set" button.

The "?" button, when pressed, displays
help about the variable.

The text field is used to enter new
values for text variables. When the
text field has keyboard focus, the
status line displays the current value
of the variable. For text variables
that apply to elements, chords or
sources, only variables that apply to
marked objects are accessed.

The "Mark" button, available only for
"Set of objects" variables, marks the
objects contained in the variable.

The "Set" button sets the variable to
the new value. For text variables, the
new value must be entered in the New
Value text field. For variables storing
sets of objects, the objects must be
marked.

For text variables that apply to
elements, sources or chords, only
variables corresponding to marked
objects will be affected.

The "Set all" button in the top row,
when pressed, has exactly the same
effect as pressing the "Set" button of
each variable except that empty new
values for variables that apply to
elements, sources or chords are
ignored. This button is only available
if the layer consists of text variables
only.

The "Reset all" button, when pressed,
resets all text fields to present
values of corresponding variables. It
is only available in layers consisting
of text variables only. The text field
is cleared for variables that apply to
elements, sources or chords when marked
objects have different values.

The "Hold" toggle button controls how
text fields for variables that apply to
elements, sources or chords are
updated. When "Hold" is disabled, these
fields are updated whenever an object
is marked or unmarked. When "Hold" is
enabled, these fields are not updated.

The "Close" button closes the dialog box.

Pressing the right mouse button over a
text field or a button associated with
a variable brings up a pop-up menu. If
the mouse has less than 3 buttons, the
menu can be displayed by pressing
Ctrl+left button.

The "Reset" command sets the text field
to the current value of the variable,
similar to the "Reset all" button but
affecting only one variable. For
variables storing sets of objects, this
command acts exactly like the "Mark"
button.

The "Display values" command displays
values of variables that apply to
elements, chords or sources next to
corresponding objects. In order to
remove these "labels", highlight any
object or use the "View|Remove labels"
command from the main menu.

Commands in the "Compare" submenu
compare values of the variable to the
value entered in the edit field and
mark matching objects.

Both the "Display values" command and
the "Compare" submenu are only
available for variables that apply to
elements, sources or chords.

---

Setup Warning dialog box

This dialog box appears if you choose
one of menu items in the Options|Setup
submenu for the first time.

This submenu should be used only by
experts. Wrong settings here can cause
loss of information, malfunction of DG
and programs processing DG output files
etc.

To proceed, press Proceed.

To cancel the operation, press Cancel.

---

Layer types dialog box

This dialog box lets you define layer
types, variables and default values of
variables.

To add a new layer type, press Add.

To modify an existing layer type, select
it in the list box and press Modify. You
can also double-click the name in the
list box.

To remove a layer type, select it in the
list box and press Remove.

To close the dialog box, press Close.

---

New layer type... dialog box

This dialog box allows you to create a
new layer type.

To create a new layer type, type it's
name in the text field and press Create.

To cancel the operation, press Cancel.

---

Layer type options dialog box

This dialog box allows you to set
attributes for a layer type and to
manage variables in this layer type.

To change the layer type attributes,
type values in text boxes and press Ok.

To add a variable, press an 'Empty'
button. The 'New variable..." dialog box
will appear. The row/column position of
the newly-created variable in the
corresponding "Variables..." dialog box
is determined by the position of the
'Empty' button pressed.

Note that in future versions of DG, only
one-column "Variables..." dialog boxes
will be supported, so it is advised to
use only the first column.

To modify or remove a variable, press a
button that displays it's name. A
"Variable options..." dialog box will
appear.

To close the dialog box without changing
layer type attributes, press the Close
button. The Close button will NOT undo
any modifications on individual
variables.

---

New variable... dialog box

This dialog box allows you to create a
new variable in a specific layer type.

To create a new variable, type it's
name in the text field and press Create.

To cancel the operation, press Cancel.

---

Variable options dialog box

This dialog box allows you to set
attributes for a variable and to remove
the variable.

To change attributes, make necessary
changes in the dialog box and press Ok.
See DG manual for details on attributes.

To change the help text associated with
the variable, press Edit Help.

To remove the variable, press Remove.

To close the dialog box without making
changes, press Cancel. The Cancel button
will NOT undo any changes made in the
Help editor dialog box.

---

Help editor dialog box

This dialog box allows you to change
the help text associated with a
variable.

To change the help text, make necessary
changes in the text field and press Ok.

To cancel the operation, press Cancel.

---

Save configuration dialog box

This dialog box requires a confirmation
for saving the current document as the
configuration file.

To confirm saving as configuration,
press Save. If there are other windows
displaying different documents, DG will
ask you whether other loaded documents
should be updated to reflect changes
made in the configuration.

To cancel the operation, press Cancel.

---

Output mode dialog box

Use this dialog box to select the output
mode and to specify which objects are
required in the output file. These
settings are saved and loaded with DG
files.

In order to specify the output mode, use
'Sonnet' and 'Carre' radio buttons.

In order to specify which objects are
required in the output file, use 'Check'
toggle buttons. Objects not required are
still exported if they exist and are
valid.

In order to accept the changes, press
'Ok'. Press 'Cancel' to close the dialog
box without making any changes.

The output mode determines the way of
editing magnetic surfaces and grid
points and the output file format.

The Sonnet output mode results in an
output file compatible with Sonnet. In
this mode, all editing operations on
surfaces and grid points are possible,
and the output file contains information
on individual surfaces and grid points.

The Carre output mode results in an
output file compatible with Carre. In
this mode, surfaces are created using a
special distribution law, and the output
file contains it's coefficients. This
imposes much tougher restrictions on
editing operations.

In Carre mode, surfaces may NOT be
edited manually, except for setting the
innermost surface. The only way of
creating surfaces is by using the
'Edit|Create|Surface(s)...' menu
command. Press the 'Help' button in the
'Create Surface(s)' dialog box for more
information.

Similarly, in Carre mode grid points may
NOT be edited manually, but must be
created using the 'Edit|Create|Grid
points(s)...' menu command.

In Carre mode, the output files can only
be produced if all surfaces and grid
points have been created in Carre mode
and did not undergo any further editing.

---

Mesh editing dialog box

Use this dialog box to specify options
for mesh editing. They only affect
one-dimensional movements of mesh
points.

"Slide along" specifies how movements of
mesh points are confined.

When "Magnetic surfaces" is in effect,
the movement is confined to a magnetic
surface passing through the original
location of the mesh point. This method
should work fine except for the vicinity
of the X point, where magnetic surfaces
drawn by DG become distorted.

When "Splines" is in effect, the
movement is confined to a spline passing
through a row of existing mesh points.
This method may be useful in the
vicinity of the X point.

"Margin" specifies the minimum distance
between adjacent mesh points. If this
limit is about to get exceeded,
adjacent points start moving in order
to "make room".

The "Double border" setting, when
enabled, instructs DG to treat outermost
mesh cells as a double border. They
would be resized together with adjacent
"regular" cells.

---

About... dialog box

Displays program version

---

Statistics dialog box

Displays statistics about the current
file.

---

ToolBox

Use the ToolBox to select tools faster
than with the toolbar.

The Close button closes the ToolBox

The Menu buttons hides or displays the
menu in the main window

The Undo and Redo buttons work exactly
like Edit|Undo and Edit|Redo menu
commands.

All other buttons are used to assign
tools to mouse buttons. To assign a
tool, press it's button using the mouse
button the tool should be assigned to.
The tools assigned to mouse buttons are
highlighted.

---

Examine tool

The Examine tool lets you display
information about objects in the work
area.

To display information about an object,
move the mouse pointer to this object
and press the mouse button the tool is
assigned to. The information is
displayed in the message line.

To display information about other
objects, move the mouse pointer to them
without releasing the mouse button.

To stop using the Examine tool, release
the mouse button. The information
displayed last will remain on the
message line.

---

Zoom/Pan tool

The Zoom/Pan tool lets you change the
zoom factor and the location$ displayed
in the work area.

To move around the drawing, move the
mouse pointer to some location in the
work area, press the mouse button the
tool is assigned to and release the
button without moving the mouse. This
location will now be displayed in the
center of the work area.

You can also "scroll" the area$
displayed in the work area. To scroll,
move the mouse pointer to some location,
press Shift+mouse button and move the
mouse to a new location. A moving
rectangle will be displayed as you move
the mouse. Finally, release the mouse
button.

To increase the zoom factor, move the
mouse pointer to some location, press
the mouse button the tool is assigned
to. Then move the mouse in order to
define a rectangle around the desired
area and release the mouse button. The
area surrounded by the rectangle will be
enlargened to fit the entire work area.

To decrease the zoom factor, press
Shift+mouse button with the mouse
pointer in the work area and release the
button without moving the mouse.

---

Mark tool

The Mark tool allows you to "mark"
elements, sources, chords, separators,
mesh elements and cells.

In order to mark or unmark a single
object, point to it, press and release
the mouse button the tool is assigned
to. Marked objects are highlighted.

In order to mark or unmark a group of
adjacent elements, move the mouse
pointer to the first of them and press
the mouse button. In order to mark or
unmark adjacent elements, move the
mouse pointer to them without releasing
the mouse button. When finished,
release the mouse button.

In order to mark or unmark a group of
elements connected by regular points,
point to an element belonging to the
group, press and release Shift+mouse
button without moving the mouse.

In order to mark a group of objects
contained within a certain rectangle,
move the mouse pointer to one corner of
the rectangle and press and hold Shift+
mouse button. Move the mouse pointer to
the opposite corner of the rectangle and
release the mouse button. If any objects
were marked before the beginning of the
operation, it will affect only objects
of the same kinds as those already
marked. Otherwise, the operation will
affect all supported kinds of objects.

---

Move tool

The "Move" allows you to visually move
objects in the work area.

To move an object, point to it and press
the mouse button the tool is assigned
to. Drag the object to the new location
and release the mouse button.

In Carre mode, you may not move
magnetic surfaces and grid points
except for redefining the innermost
magnetic surface in the main plasma. In
the latter case, all other surfaces in
the area will be deleted.

---

Delete tool

The Delete tool lets you delete objects.

To delete an object, point to it and
press the mouse button the tool is
assigned to. Then release the mouse
button.

In Carre mode, you may not delete
surfaces and grid points. You can still
use the 'Edit|Delete|...' submenu to
delete these objects.

---

Add Element tool

The Add Element tool allows you to
create elements.

To create an element attached to an
existing point, move the mouse pointer to
this point and press the mouse button the
tool is assigned to. Without releasing
the mouse button, move the mouse pointer
to the desired location of the other end
of the element and release the mouse
button.

To create an element without attaching
it to an existing point, move the mouse
pointer to the desired location of the
beginning of the element and press
Shift+mouse button. Without releasing
the mouse button, move the pointer to
the desired location of the other end
and release the mouse button.

If there are no points defined, this
tool will behave as with Shift+mouse
button.

---

Add Source tool

The Add Source tool lets you create
sources.

To create a source, point to it's desired
location and press the mouse button the
tool is assigned to. By moving the mouse,
you can adjust the position of the
source. When finished, release the mouse
button.

---

Add Chord tool

The Add Chord tool allows you to create
chords.

To create a chord, move the mouse pointer
to the desired location of it's beginning
and press the mouse button the tool is
assigned to. Without releasing the mouse
button, move the mouse pointer to the
desired location of the other end of the
chord and release the mouse button.

---

Add Surface tool

The Add Surface tool lets you create
magnetic surfaces.

An equilibrium must be imported before
defining surfaces.

To create a magnetic surface, point to
some location inside an equilibrium
zone and press the mouse button the
tool is assigned to. By moving the
mouse, you can adjust the position of
the surface. When finished, release the
mouse button.

In Carre mode, you may not add any
surfaces except for creating a surface
in an empty main plasma area.

You can also add "virtual" surfaces,
which can exist outside of defined
equilibrium zones and cannot be written
into the output file. In order to
create a virtual surface, press
Shift+mouse button inside the
equilibrium. By moving the mouse, you
can adjust the position of the surface.
When finished, release the mouse
button.

---

Add Grid Point tool

The Add Grid Point tool lets you create
surfaces.

An equilibrium must be imported and the X
point defined before creating grid
points.

To create a grid point, point to it's
desired location on the separatrix and
press the mouse button the tool is
assigned to. By moving the mouse, you can
adjust the position of the grid point.
When finished, release the mouse button.

In Carre mode, you may not add any grid
points using this tool.

---

Set X Point tool

The Set X Point tool lets you define the
X point.

An equilibrium must be imported and
targets intersecting the separatrix must
be defined before using this tool.

To define the X point, you must define a
rectangle around it. Point to the first
corner of the desired rectangle and
press the mouse button the tool is
assigned to. Move the mouse pointer to
the opposite corner and release the
mouse button.

In case of success, the separatrix will
be displayed. Otherwise, an error
message will appear on the message line.

---

Split Element tool

The Split Element tool allows you to
split an element in two.

To split an element, point to it and
press the mouse button the tool is
assigned to. By moving the mouse, you
can adjust the position of the
newly-created point. When finished,
release the mouse button.

In the current version of DG, variables
belonging to the original element are
not copied.

---

Join Elements tool

The Join Elements tool allows you to
join two elements connected by a regular
point into one.

To join two elements, move the mouse
pointer to the point between them, press
and release the mouse button the tool is
assigned to.

In the current version of DG, variables
belonging to the original two elements
are not preserved.

---

Connect Points tool

The Connect Points tool allows you to
create an element between two
pre-defined points.

To connect two points, point to the
first of them and press the mouse button
the tool is assigned to. Move the mouse
pointer to the second point and release
the mouse button.

---

Reposition tool

The Reposition tool allows you to
re-attach an existing element or
separator to a different point, or to
reposition the template by attaching it
to existing points.

In order to re-attach an element, point
to one of it's ends and press the mouse
button the tool is assigned to. Move the
mouse pointer to the desired point and
release the mouse button.

In order to re-attach a separator, use
the similar procedure except that only
one end of a separator can be
re-attached.

In order to reposition the template,
point somewhere on the template and
press and hold Shift+mouse button. Move
the mouse pointer to some existing point
and release the mouse button. The
template will be moved, rescaled and
rotated so that the specified point on
the template ("handle") will be moved to
the selected point.

In order to precisely position the
template, new positions should be
specified for two handles on it. The
first time you use the Reposition tool
on the template ("first handle"), the
template is only moved. The next time
("second handle"), the template is
rotated and rescaled as well, so that
the first handle remains at the
previously specified position. The next
time, the template is again only moved,
and so on...

---

Reverse Normals tool

The Reverse Normals tool allows you to
reverse elements and their external
normals.

In order to reverse an element, point to
it and press the mouse button the tool
is assigned to. By moving the mouse
pointer to adjacent elements, you can
force their normals to point to the same
direction. When finished, release the
mouse button.

In order to reverse a group of elements
connected by regular points, point to
one of the elements and press
Shift+mouse button. Then release the
mouse button.

---

Move mesh point tool

This tool allows you to adjust mesh
points.

In order to move a mesh point along the
magnetic surface, point to it and press
the mouse button. Drag the mesh point
with the mouse to the new location.
Then, release the mouse button.

In order to move a mesh point without
staying on the same magnetic surface,
point to it and press Shift+mouse
button. Drag the mesh point with the
mouse to the new location. Then, release
the mouse button.

Use the "Options|Mesh editing..." menu
command to set options for this tool.

---

File|New menu command

This command opens a new window
containig a new file. If the current
window is empty, nothing happens.

---

File|Open... menu command

This command brings up the Open...
dialog box which lets you load existing
DG files.

---

File|Recent|... menu commands

These menu commands allow opening files
most recently accessed by DG without
going through the Open... dialog box.

---

File|Save... menu command

This command brings up the Save...
dialog box which lets you save the file
in the current window.

---

File|Output... menu command

This command generates output files. A
confirmation dialog box will appear.
Press Ok to create the files.

The way output files are created depends
from the output mode selected from the
'Options|Output mode' submenu.

---

File|Import|Template... menu command

This command brings up the Template...
dialog box allowing you to import a
template.

---

File|Import|Equilibrium... menu command

This command brings up the Equilibrium...
dialog box allowing you to import an
equilibrium.

---

File|Import|Mesh... menu command

This command brings up the Mesh...
dialog box allowing you to import a
mesh.

---

File|Import|Topology... menu command

This command brings up the "Import
Topology" dialog box allowing you to
import a topology from another DG file.

---

File|Print... menu command

This command brings up the Print...
dialog box allowing you to print the
contents of the current window.

---

File|Exit program menu command

This command closes all windows and
exits DG.

If some windows contain unsaved work,
you will be prompted either to save it
or to discard it.

The effect of this command can NOT be
reversed by Undo.

---

File|Export|Mesh... menu command

This command brings up the "Export
Mesh..." dialog box allowing you to
export the mesh, normally after having
modified it in DG.

---

File|Export|Elements as Template... menu command

This command brings up the "Export
Elements as Template" dialog box
allowing you to export elements into a
template file.

---

Edit|Undo menu command

This command reverses the effect of the
last operation in the current file.
Multiple Undo commands will reverse
effects of multiple consequent
operations.

---

Edit|Redo menu command

This command reverses the effect of the
last Undo command, if no other
operations have been done. Multiple Redo
commands will reverse effects of
multiple consequent Undo commands.

---

Edit|Redo all command

This command is similar to the Redo
command except that it reverses the
effect of multiple Undo commands at
once.

---

Edit|Mark all elements command

This command marks all elements. Marked
sources are not affected.

---

Edit|Unmark all menu command

This commands unmarks all objects.

---

Edit|Create|Point... menu command

This commands brings up the Create
Point... dialog box.

---

Edit|Create|Source... menu command

This commands brings up the Create
Source... dialog box.

---

Edit|Create|Chord... menu command

This commands brings up the Create
Chord... dialog box.

---

Edit|Create|Surface... menu command

This commands brings up the Create
Surface... dialog box.

---

Edit|Create|Grid point(s)... menu command

This commands brings up the Create Grid
Point(s)... dialog box.

---

Edit|Create|Separators menu command

This commands creates separators between
the mesh and the structure.

The structure must be properly
positioned in order to make it possible
to create separators without
intersecting the structure or the mesh.

---

Edit|Delete|Unused points menu command

This command deletes all points that
have no elements attached.

---

Edit|Delete|Virtual surfaces menu command

This command deletes all virtual surfaces.

---

Edit|Delete|Marked objects menu command

This command deletes all marked objects.

---

Edit|Delete|Elements menu command

This command deletes all elements.

---

Edit|Delete|Sources menu command

This command deletes all sources.

---

Edit|Delete|Chords menu command

This command deletes all chords.

---

Edit|Delete|Surface menu command

This command deletes all surfaces.

---

Edit|Delete|Grid points menu command

This command deletes all grid points.

---

Edit|Delete|Separators menu command

This command deletes all separators.

---

Edit|Delete|X point menu command

This command deletes the X point.

---

Edit|Delete|Equilibrium menu command

This command unloads the equilibrium.

---

Edit|Delete|Template menu command

This command unloads the template.

---

Edit|Delete|Mesh menu command

This command unloads the mesh.

---

Edit|Move/Rotate... menu command

This command brings up the
Move/Rotate... dialog box.

---

View|Refresh menu command

This command redraws the contents of the
work area.

It appears in the menu only when manual
refresh is enabled.

---

View|Zoom In menu command

This command increases the zoom factor.

---

View|Zoom Out menu command

This command decreases the zoom factor.

---

View|Picture view menu command

This command adjusts the zoom factor so
that all visible objects fit into the
work area.

---

View|Selection view menu command

This command adjusts the zoom factor so
that all selected objects fit into the
work area.

---

View|Previous menu command

This menu command restores the previous
zoom factor and visible area. It can be
used to reverse the effect of zoom
operations while leaving any changes
made to the model intact.

When issued multiple times in a
sequence, this command follows the
command history, thus allowing reversing
the effect of multiple zoom operations.

---

View|Remove labels menu command

This menu command removes all labels
from objects.

---

View|Display|... toggle buttons

These toggle buttons allow you to hide
or display different types of objects.

---

View|Mesh header... menu command

This menu command brings up the "Mesh
header" dialog box, allowing you to
view and modify the header of the
current mesh file.

---

Commands|Convert|Template to elements menu command

This command creates a set of elements
from the template.

Duplicate elements are not produced.
After the completion, the template
remains in memory.

---

Commands|Convert|Elements to chords menu command

This command converts marked elements
into chords.

Duplicate chords are not produced.
Original elements are deleted.

---

Commands|Convert|Chords to elements menu command

This command converts marked chords into
elements.

Duplicate elements are not produced.
Original chords are deleted.

---

Commands|Simplify|Fill gaps... menu command

This command brings up the Fill Gaps
dialog box, which allows you to fill
gaps between elements. It can be used to
automatically correct problems with
converted templates.

---

Commands|Simplify|Line up normals|... menu commands

These commands force external normals of
adjacent elements to point to the same
direction. 'All' affects all elements,
while 'Marked' only affects marked ones.
These menu commands can be used to
automatically correct problems with
converted templates.

---

Commands|Simplify|Merge/Split elements... menu command

This command brings up the 'Merge/Split
elements' dialog box, which allows you
to control the number of elements while
producing only minimal disturbances to
the geometry.

---

Commands|Renumber elements menu command

This command renumbers elements and
separators so that the output file does
not contain dummy values.

---

Commands|Check variables menu command

This menu command allows you to locate
all variables that have invalid  or no
values. It will bring up the Invalid
Values dialog box that lets you correct
these errors in a very convenient way.

This command should be used after the
File|Output... menu command reports
'Some variables have invalid values'.

---

Commands|Rebuild Carre objects menu command

This menu command attemts to rebuild
surfaces and grid points using the
previously used coefficients. It is only
available in Carre mode.

The main purpose of this command is to
automatically adjust surfaces after
changes in the innermost surface or the
structure.

---

Commands|Edit topology... menu command

This menu command brings up the "Edit
topology" dialog box, allowing you to
manually define the topology.

---

Variables|Add ... menu command

This command creates a layer of
variables.

---

Variables|Remove ... menu command

This command destroys a layer of
variables.


---

Variables| ... menu command

This command displays a layer of
variables.

---

Options|Display menu toggle button

This toggle button allows you to hide or
display the menu in the main window.

When the menu is not displayed, a button
"Menu" appears in the work area. By
pressing this button, the menu can be
re-displayed.

Note: when the menu is not displayed,
keyboard accelerators for menu commands
do not work.

---

Options|Output mode... menu command

This menu command brings up the Output
Mode dialog box, which allows to set the
output mode and to specify which
information is required for the output
file.

---

Options|Mesh editing... menu command

This menu command brings up the "Mesh
Editing" dialog box, which allows to
specify options for the mesh editing
process.

---

Options|Display toolbar toggle button

This toggle button allows you to hide or
display the toolbar.

---

Options|Manual refresh toggle button

This toggle button allows you to enable
or disable the manual refresh.

With the manual refesh enabled, DG does
not redraw the contents of the work area
automatically when a previously-hidden
part of the window becomes exposed or
after small editing operations.

With this option enabled, the Refresh
command appears in the View menu. This
command lets you redraw the window
contents.

---

Options|Program setup... menu command

This command should NOT be used by
non-experienced users. Wrong settings
here may prevent DG or other programs
from working correctly.

This command brings up the Setup...
dialog box.

This dialog box allows you to customize
DG variables.

---

Options|Setup|Variables... menu command

This command will bring up the Layer
Types dialog box.

This dialog box allows you to customize
layers and manage individual variables.

---

Options|Setup|Save... menu command

This command will save the file in the
current window as the configuration
file. It will also update all other
files loaded in DG to this
configuration.

Use with care - wrong settings can cause
loss of information and conflicts with
other programs.

---

Window|New view menu command

This command opens a new window
containing the same file as the current
window.

---

Window|Close menu command

This command closes the current window.
If the window contains unsaved work that
is not displayed in other windows, you
will be prompted either to save or to
discard the file.

The effect of this command can NOT be
reversed by Undo.

---

Window|Statistics menu command

This command brings up the
Statistics dialog box.

This dialog box displays statistics on
objects in the current file.

---

Window|Toolbox menu command

This command brings up the ToolBox.

The ToolBox is more convenient, but less
standard tool for assigning tools to
mouse buttons, than the toolbar.

---

Help menu

This menu lets you access the help
system.

The 'What's New' menu command displays
the revision history of DG.

The 'Manual' menu command displays the
online version of the manual.

The 'Using help' menu command displays
instructions on using the help system.

The 'On main window' menu command
displays help on the main window.

---

Help|About... menu command

This command displays program version.

---

DG Manual

The online manual is not available in this
version of DG.

---

What's New

Here is a brief list of changes in DG:

Version 2.01, March 04, 2004 BETA
------------------------------------

- New: the mesh filename is added to the *.trg output file


Version 2.0, October 12, 1998 BETA
------------------------------------

- New: multiple-null topology support.
  - More than 1 X point allowed.
  - X points, separatrix sections and equilibrium zones can be manually
    defined using the "Commanda|Edit topology..." menu command.
  - The topology can also be imported from an existing DG file using the
    "File|Import|Topology..." menu command, in most cases.
  - Magnetic surfaces and grid points now "survive" loading a different
    equilibrium and reappear once a topology has been imported/created.
    Magnetic surfaces retain the same "zone" and "level" values.
  - No need to remove X point(s) prior to modifying targets.
  - Magnetic surfaces can now only be created inside equilibrium zones,
    as defined in the topology.
  - "Virtual" surfaces can be created anywhere in the equilibrium by 
    using Shift+Add Surface tool. Virtual surfaces cannot be exported.
  - "Set X Point" tool no longer available. X points now defined in the 
    topology.
  - The "Move" tool can no longer "stretch" grid points in a section.
  - "Edit|Delete|X Point" menu command no longer available. Individual X
    points can be deleted with the "Delete" tool while editing topology.
  - In "Create multiple..." dialog boxes, you now specify the number of 
    radial and poloidal cells instead of the number of magnetic surfaces 
    and grid points.
  - The "Statistics" dialog box now counts cells instead of magnetic 
    surfaces and grid points.
  - A single-null topology must be imported into DG 1.x files in order to
    see magnetic surfaces and grid points.

- Interface
  - "View|Mesh header..." now sensitive.
  - Some menu items from "File" moved into "File|Import".


Version 1.42, March 5, 1998
---------------------------

- New
  - Variables in the output file are now sorted according to their
    positions in the "Variables" dialog box.


Version 1.41, March 3, 1998
---------------------------

- New
  - Mesh file headers now preserved when exporting a mesh file.
  - "View|Mesh header..." menu command for viewing and editing them.
  - "File|Export|Elements as Template..." menu command and dialog box.

- Interface
  - "View|Previous" renamed to "View|Previous view".
  - New environment variables.
    - DG_EXPORT_TEMPLATE_MASK for exporting templates.
    - DG_EXPORT_MESH_MASK     for exporting meshes.


Version 1.4b, January 21, 1998
------------------------------

- Bugs fixed
  - DG sometimes crashed when producing a Carre mode output file.
  - The "Examine" tool didn't work with "Display Mesh" enabled.
  - "Split elements" produced zero-length elements.
  - Wrong filename was sometimes highlighted in the "Save..." dialog box.


Version 1.4a, December 9, 1997
------------------------------

- Bugs fixed
  - DG crashed on SGI, some other systems.
  - Some DG files took too long to load.
  - Memory leak in the menu system.
  - Missing error messages in the Mesh Options dialog box.


Version 1.4, November 2, 1997
-----------------------------

- Mesh editing added.
  - "Move Mesh Point" tool.
  - "Export Mesh" menu command.
  - Modifications to the mesh saved into DG files.
  - Separators temporarily disabled.
  - "Options|Mesh editing..." menu command.
  - "View|Display|Mesh details" toggle button.
  - Mesh objects can now be examined.
  - Mesh cells and elements can now be marked.
  - New types of variables for storing sets of mesh cells and elements.

- Interface
  - New "Variables" dialog box
    - More compact layout.
    - "Compare" function.
    - "Display values" function.
    - The dialog can be resized and the content scrolled.
    - The dialog can be rolled up/down with a single mouse click.
    - Improved error checking.
  - Warnings during loading files are now displayed again.
  - "Display" submenu restructured
  - The contrast of equilibrium display enhanced.

- Bugs fixed
  - Torn-off menus caused a coredump on exit/close window.
  - Attempt to create a surface on a horizontal plane caused a coredump.
  - Memory leaks in the "variables" dialog box.
  - "Mark group" worked even with element display turned off.
  - "File|Output..." locked up if there were no elements/separators.

- Problems
  - Mesh operations do not work on SGI workstations
  - The "Move mesh point" tool is not completely foolproof.
  - The field ratio for modified mesh cells is sometimes recalculated
    with a big error.


Version 1.34a, September 9, 1997
--------------------------------

- Interface
  - Windows are now properly de-iconified when necessary.
  - 'Show picture' now considers magnetic surfaces and grid points instead
    of using the equilibrium boundary.
  - The margin for 'Show picture' reduced.
  - A darker color is now used for grid labels.

- Bugfixes
  - Layer label variables caused crashes on SGI.
  - Wrong window title in the help dialog.
  - 'Show picture' did not interpret the mesh correctly.


Version 1.34, August 29, 1997
-----------------------------

- New
  - The "Output mode" dialog box allows to determine which items are
    required in the output file.
  - The DG_NO_OUTPUT_CHECK environment variable is no longer supported.
  - "Layer label" option for variables.

- Interface
  - "Variables" menu restructured.
  - Pressing the Ok button now closes the "New version dialog box.
  - "Output mode" submenu replaced with a dialog box.

- Bugfixes
  - Wrong number of chains was output into the structure file in Carre mode.


Version 1.33, August 25, 1997
-----------------------------

- New
  - 'Edit|Delete|Marked objects' menu command
  - The DG_NO_OUTPUT_CHECK environment variable, when set, disables all
    checks when producing the output file. Note that DG works much less
    reliably in this mode.


Version 1.32, August 22, 1997
-----------------------------

- New
  - Import HPGL files as templates (only subset of HPGL records supported).
  - The template can now be scaled and rotated by any angle.
  - Elements can be converted into chords and vice versa.
  - The Reposition tool can be used to visually position the template.
  - Chords
    - Can now be marked.
    - Can now have variables (Scope:Chords)
    - Can now be included in variables (Type:Set of chords)

- Changes
  - Element numbers of deleted elements are now re-cycled.

- Interface
  - Chords now listed in the statistics.
  - 'Mark' tool now allows marking inside a rectangle.
  - 'Commands' menu reorganized.
  - The current file is now selected in the Save... dialog box.
  - Scaling permitted in the Move/Rotate dialog box.

- Bugfixes
  - Shift+Click in the zoom tool did not work as documented.
  - Adding a marked source to "set of elements" variables crashed DG.
  - Some problems with reading very old files corrected.
  - Attempt to load a non-existing file sometimes caused a coredump.
  - Fixed minor problems in the "New version" dialog box.
  - The "Show Picture" command worked incorrectly with templates.
  - Trying to put a marked source into a variable crashed DG.


Version 1.31c, July 25, 1997
----------------------------

- Bugs fixed
  - Phantom sources appeared when loading a file with surfaces.


Version 1.31b, July 2, 1997
---------------------------

- Changes
  - Output file items renamed:
    - 'xpointcenter' to 'xptcntr'
    - 'xloopcenter'  to 'xlpcntr'


Version 1.31a, May 13, 1997
---------------------------

- Changes
  - 'closedstr' entry in targets file renamed to 'clstruct'.
  - 'Rebuild Carre Objects' now erases all non-Carre grid points.


Version 1.31, May 9, 1997
-------------------------

- Changes
  - Surfaces in Carre mode now forced to intersect parts of structure
    containing targets.
  - Non-closed parts of structure now allowed in Carre mode.
  - It is now allowed to output 0 grid points in Carre mode.

- Interface
  - Window|ToolBox menu command automatically hides the toolbar.

- Bugs fixed
  - Loading files with automatically created surfaces sometimes crashed DG.
  - Equilibrium/Template/Mesh files sometimes not loaded with the DG file.
  - DG_MESH_MASK environment varialbe did not have any effect.
  - 'OK' button improperly greyed out in Create Surface(s) dialog box.


Version 1.3 - April 30, 1997
----------------------------

- Carre output mode added
  - Options|Output mode submenu for selecting the output mode
  - New file format, due to the output mode
  - No bounding rectangle around the structure is produced in Carre mode
  - Creation parameters of surfaces/grid points are output in Carre mode
  - Manual editing of surfaces/grid points is not permitted in Carre mode
  - 'Commands|Rebuild Carre objects' menu command added

- Added
  - Preferences file
  - Recent files list
  - Improved creation of multiple surfaces and grid points
    - The curve can be now dragged with the mouse
    - New "Delta" law added

- Changes
  - Environment variables renamed
    - DGOPENMASK     to DG_OPEN_MASK
    - DGSAVEMASK     to DG_SAVE_MASK
    - DGEQUILMASK    to DG_EQUIL_MASK
    - DGTEMPLATEMASK to DG_TEMPLATE_MASK
    - DGSONNETMASK   to DG_MESH_MASK
  - It is now allowed to output 0 grid points in Sonnet mode

- Interface
  - Stopped echoing filenames when started with filename parameters
  - DG version change notification added
  - Color of enabled toggle buttons changed to yellow
  - 'Get info/Information' dialog box renamed to Statistics
  - 'Sonnet output' object renamed to 'Mesh'
  - 'Help|Manual' menu item removed

- Bugs fixed
  - Help on main window not displayed properly
  - Undo history was not cleared after a start with an empty command line
  - The help dialog box sometimes contained all help topics together
  - Some memory leaks removed


Version 1.26 - April 3, 1997
----------------------------

- Added
  - 'Commands|Simplify|...' submenu for post-processing converted templates
  - Shift+'Reverse Normals' tool now reverses a whole group of elements
  - 'View|Previous' menu command restores recent zoom factors
  - Default values for variables now taken from the configuration
  - 'Help|What's New' menu command displays the revision history

- Interface
  - Convert template now enables the display of irregular points and normals
  - Removed mnemonics from the tools menu
  - Opening template/equil/sonnet output dialogs shows existing objects
  - Removed double top margin from most dialog boxes
  - Hotkey for manual refresh changed from ^R to F5

- Bugs fixed
  - DG sometimes crashed after PostScript output
  - DG crashed on Sun's
  - DG sometimes crashed when the Vars menu contained no sensitive items
  - Help text for variables was not taken from the configuration sometimes
  - Minor problems in dialog boxes


Version 1.25 - March 17, 1997
-----------------------------

- New
  - Create surface by area/level
  - Create multiple surfaces
  - Create multiple grid points (very enhanced)

- Interface
  - Faster creation of dialog boxes

- Bugs fixed
  - DG crashed on Delete X Point when not all surfaces intersected targets
  - Mark Group caused a lock-up in some configurations


Version 1.24
------------

- New
  - X point and peak coordinates in the output file

- Internal
  - Some optimizations - many operations are now times faster
  - Multiple X points skeleton (in progress)


Version 1.23
------------

- New
  - PostScript output ('File|Print...' menu command)
  - 'Commands|Check variables' menu command
  - "Non-strict" rules
    - Deleted elements automatically removed from variables
    - Surfaces/X-Point automatically removed on Delete Equilibrium
    - Separators automatically removed on Delete Sonnet Grid

- Bugs fixed
  - Delete Equil/SonnetGrid did not display error message when needed
  - Some crashes caused by wrong pointers

- Internal
  - New file format


Version 1.22
------------

- New
  - Chords

- Interface
  - Display toggle buttons moved to the View|Display submenu
  - "Program setup" dialog replaced with a submenu
  - Non-modal setup dialog boxes
  - "Add Element" tool: Shift+Button does not attach to an existing point
  - Help topics for all setup dialog boxes
  - Help buttons in most setup dialog boxes.
  - "No popup" toggle button in the Help window menu
  - Error box displayed in case of invalid paths in DG...MASK variables
  - Save Configuration dialog is now modeless and simpler
  - Version information now displayed on the status line after startup.
  - Changed the layout of the About... dialog.

- Bugs fixed
  - Sources were not relevant for View|Picture View
  - Edit|Remove|Unused points was not greyed properly
  - Help buttons did not work in some dialog boxes
  - Minor internal bugs

- Internal
  - New file format


Version 1.21
------------

- New
  - Broken 2-column template file format

- Interface
  - Accelerators for Undo/Redo buttons
  - Changed drawing order of objects
  - Only zoom/flags change is no more considered 'unsaved document'
  - Blue component in the background color added

- Bugs fixed
  - Missing strings and wrong help topics in Motif resources
  - Added a scrollbar to the list of layer types


Version 1.20
------------

- New
  - Online help
  - Manual refresh mode ('Options|Manual refresh' menu command)
  - Menu and/or toolbar can be disabled separately  ('Options|...')
  - 'Options/mark groups removed'; groups now marked with Shift+Mark

- Bugs fixed
  - Online help system sometimes caused an exception
  - Online help system displayed an extra line in the help window

